### What is the core design?

The Composite pattern is the heart of the design. An abstract `FileSystemNode` declares operations that both files and directories support. A `File` is a leaf. A `Directory` is a composite that holds children.

```java
package com.bank.fs.model;

public abstract class FileSystemNode {
    private final String name;
    private Permission permission;
    private Directory parent;

    protected FileSystemNode(String name, Permission permission) {
        this.name = name;
        this.permission = permission;
    }

    public abstract long size();
    public abstract void delete();

    public String getName() { return name; }
    public Permission getPermission() { return permission; }
    public Directory getParent() { return parent; }
    public void setParent(Directory parent) { this.parent = parent; }

    public String getPath() {
        if (parent == null) return "/" + name;
        return parent.getPath() + "/" + name;
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.fs.model;

public class Permission {
    private final boolean read;
    private final boolean write;
    private final boolean execute;

    public Permission(boolean read, boolean write, boolean execute) {
        this.read = read;
        this.write = write;
        this.execute = execute;
    }

    public boolean canRead() { return read; }
    public boolean canWrite() { return write; }
    public boolean canExecute() { return execute; }
}
```

### How does the File leaf work?

A `File` is a leaf that stores content and returns its own byte size.

```java
package com.bank.fs.model;

public class File extends FileSystemNode {
    private byte[] content;

    public File(String name, Permission permission, byte[] content) {
        super(name, permission);
        this.content = content;
    }

    @Override
    public long size() {
        return content.length;
    }

    @Override
    public void delete() {
        System.out.println("Deleted file: " + getPath());
    }

    public byte[] getContent() { return content; }
}
```

### How does the Directory composite work?

A `Directory` holds child nodes and computes its size by summing its children recursively.

```java
package com.bank.fs.model;

import java.util.*;

public class Directory extends FileSystemNode {
    private final Map<String, FileSystemNode> children;

    public Directory(String name, Permission permission) {
        super(name, permission);
        this.children = new LinkedHashMap<>();
    }

    public void addChild(FileSystemNode node) {
        if (children.containsKey(node.getName())) {
            throw new IllegalStateException(
                    "Node " + node.getName() + " already exists");
        }
        node.setParent(this);
        children.put(node.getName(), node);
    }

    public void removeChild(String name) {
        children.remove(name);
    }

    public FileSystemNode getChild(String name) {
        return children.get(name);
    }

    @Override
    public long size() {
        return children.values().stream()
                .mapToLong(FileSystemNode::size)
                .sum();
    }

    @Override
    public void delete() {
        for (FileSystemNode child : new ArrayList<>(children.values())) {
            child.delete();
        }
        children.clear();
        System.out.println("Deleted directory: " + getPath());
    }

    public Collection<FileSystemNode> getChildren() {
        return children.values();
    }
}
```

